%U A401831 796675461120000,24856274386944000,753973656403968000,22619209692119040000,158334467844833280000
%N A401831 Number of permutations of {1..n} such that no two primes are adjacent.
%C A401831 Formula explanation: Arrange the c = n - pi(n) nonprimes in c! ways, creating c + 1 gaps. Choose p = pi(n) gaps in binomial(c + 1, p) ways and place the p primes in them in p! ways, giving a(n) = c! * binomial(c + 1, p) * p! = c! * (c + 1)! / (c - p + 1)!.
%H A401831 Mykola Filipchuk, <a href="/A401831/b401831.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..453</a>
%F A401831 a(n) = (n - pi(n))! * (n - pi(n) + 1)! / (n - 2*pi(n) + 1)!, where pi = A000720.
%F A401831 a(n) = c! * binomial(c + 1, p) * p! = c! * (c + 1)! / (c - p + 1)!, where p = pi(n) and c = n - p.
%e A401831 For n = 3, the primes {2, 3} must be separated by the nonprime 1, giving a(3) = 2 valid permutations: (2, 1, 3) and (3, 1, 2).
%e A401831 For n = 4, the nonprimes {1, 4} can be arranged in 2! = 2 ways, creating 3 gaps [_ x _ y _] into which the 2 primes {2, 3} can be placed in 3*2 = 6 ways, giving a(4) = 2 * 6 = 12.
%p A401831 a:= n-> (p-> (n-p)!*(n-p+1)!/(n-2*p+1)!)(numtheory[pi](n)):
%p A401831 seq(a(n), n=0..23);
%o A401831 (Python)
%o A401831 from math import factorial
%o A401831 from sympy import primepi
%o A401831 def a(n):
%o A401831     p = primepi(n)
%o A401831     c = n - p
%o A401831     return factorial(c) * factorial(c + 1) // factorial(c - p + 1)
%o A401831 print([a(n) for n in range(0, 24)])
%o A401831 (PARI)
%o A401831 a(n) = my(p=primepi(n), c=n-p); c! * (c+1)! / (c-p+1)!;
%o A401831 vector(24, n, a(n-1))
%Y A401831 Cf. A000142, A000720, A062298, A258213, A401019.
